{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat docs/design/datom.world.md'","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Core philosophy, foundational axioms, invariants, primitives (tuples, streams, host boundaries), architectural commitments, design principles, code style, and development workflow for datom.world\n---\n\n# DATOM.WORLD ARCHITECTURAL FOUNDATIONS & DEVELOPMENT RULES\n\n## Core Philosophy\n\nEverything is data.\nInterpretation > abstraction.\nExplicit causality > implicit assumptions.\nRestrictions are a feature.\n\n### Foundational Axioms\n\n1. **Everything is a Stream**: Portable I/O and system data flow through append-only streams; hosts may also install explicit local functions that perform direct I/O.\n2. **Interpretation Creates Semantics**: Data is syntax; semantics only emerge through interpretation (one truth, many perspectives).\n   At system scale, a \"system\" is an emergent property of interpreters of\n   pure data, not an object, service, or stored artifact. Find the\n   interpreters and the data they share to find the system.\n3. **Code and State are Datoms**: Code (Universal AST) and runtime execution state are structured datoms, enabling syntax independence and structural reflection.\n4. **Everything is a Continuation**: Computation is modeled as serializable, portable continuations that can pause, travel across streams, and resume anywhere.\n\n## Non-Negotiable Invariants\n\n- Do not introduce hidden global state.\n- Do not introduce implicit control flow.\n- Do not introduce callbacks; every callback is an event on a stream.\n- Do not introduce shared mutable state.\n- Do not collapse interpretation and execution into the same layer.\n- Do not assume graphs: graphs must be constructed explicitly from tuples.\n- Do not lose information (unitarity): streams are append-only and no datom is destroyed, only reinterpreted. The Universal AST is never lost: it is always retrievable wherever code is lowered, by address or by a query over its datoms. Every derived form (semantic vector, native images, indexes) is an interpretation of it and may drop facts only because the AST remains.\n\n## Tuples and Datoms\n\nTuples are elements in an open moduli space, graded by dimension $n$. A tuple can be any dimension/size.\nA datom is the canonical persistent tuple `[e a v t m]` (entity, attribute, value, transaction, metadata). Persistent facts are immutable datoms.\nSource scope belongs to the interpreter, never to an appended tuple slot. Queries keep physical sources as separate db-values and express union or joins explicitly.\n\nSee [`docs/design/datom.md`](./datom.md) for full specification.\n\n## Streams\n\n`dao.stream` is an I/O monad and the system's portable I/O ABI. Functions consume streams and produce streams.\nHosts may install arbitrary local functions, including direct I/O; their availability and migration semantics must be explicit.\nPortable behavior cannot assume them; portable side effects appear as stream emissions. Direct-I/O host modules are explicitly local and non-migratable (owner decision, 2026-10-23).\nStreams are values that can be sent through streams.\nStreams carry whatever values the consumer needs: no datom requirement, no privileged payload shape.\n\nA stream has no privileged reader, and its meaning is not in it. Any number\nof interpreters may observe the same medium, and each constructs its own\nsemantics from the same batches: a `yin.vm` evaluator observing a program\nstream constructs CESK state and executes it; `dao.space.index` observing\nthe very same stream materializes covered indexes, so `dao.space.query/q`\nanswers Datalog over the program as it grows. Neither knows the other\nexists. The medium is their only coupling, and each observer's ignorance of\nthe other is load-bearing: the evaluator never consults an index to run,\nand the index never evaluates a form to index it. This is how axiom 2\nbecomes architecture: one stream, many interpretations, none of them\nthe stream's own.\n\nFunctions can take anything and return anything.\nAgents are functions, closures, or continuations in `yin.vm` that communicate portably with the outside world through `dao.stream`. Portable agent behavior is specified through stream effects; host-local functions may also perform direct I/O.\n\nSee [`docs/design/dao.stream.md`](./dao.stream.md) for full stream specification.\nSee [`docs/design/yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md`](./yin.vm.streams-all-the-way-down.md) for VM architecture.\n\n### Host Boundaries\n\nA host boundary is the dao.stream abstraction boundary; implementations do not add contracts.\n[datom.world.abi.md](./datom.world.abi.md) owns the doctrine, owner rulings, taxonomy, floor and custody rules.\n\n## Architectural Commitments\n\n- **`dao.jing` is syntax, agents are semantics**: the content-addressed encoding stays passive and payload-agnostic.\n- **Macros are stream topology**: an evaluator knows nothing about macro expansion; expansion happens on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of any evaluator.\n- **Peer observers, one stream**: a generic evaluator and `dao.space` independently observe the same stream topic, as described under Streams. This is not a claim about any two consumers regardless of what they consume.\n- **Observers are independent and optional**: nothing requires a medium to have any particular set of consumers. Which observers exist for a stream is a per-composition choice, not a design-time commitment.\n\n## Design Principles\n\nCode quality is measured by malleability: how cheaply can changes adapt without breaking promises?\nStigmergic coordination emerges from sharing data across streams.\n\n**Systems emerge from interpreters of pure data.** Streams are the\ncomposition medium; each interpreter gives the same data a perspective\nwithout collapsing interpretation into execution or owning the truth.\n\n- `dao.space` emerges from `dao.space.index` and `dao.space.query` over\n  ordinary streams.\n- ShiBi capability, metering, and budget emerge from different\n  interpreters over the same tuples; no token object is stored.\n- The four VMs interpret one Universal AST; many syntaxes feed that AST.\n- The semantic AST in `dao.space` is queryable facts projected by the\n  auto-index observer.\n- Client and server are roles in one stigmergic behavior implemented by\n  interpreters, not distinct kinds of nodes.\n\n**Derive, don't persist.** Before adding a tag, slot, or field to any\ncanonical content-hashed structure (an AST grammar, a datom schema, a row\nshape), check whether a correctly scoped Datalog query over\n`dao.space.query/q` can already compute the fact. If it can, it belongs as a\nquery or projection: new persisted structure migrates every downstream\nconsumer and duplicates authority over a fact a query derives fresh. The\nretired `:global` tag in\n[`docs/design/yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md`](./yin.vm.code-as-tuples.md) is the\ncase study, including the scoping bug that a naive query introduced.\n\nSee [`docs/agents/malleability.md`](../agents/malleability.md) for complete design guidance (cohesion, coupling, bounded complexity, evolution, testing strategy).\nSee [`docs/agents/vocabulary.md`](../agents/vocabulary.md) for domain vocabulary reference (biology, economics, physics, philosophy).\n\n## Code Style\n\n- Prefer simple data over rich types.\n- Prefer total functions over partial ones.\n- Prefer declarative pipelines over imperative logic.\n- Avoid cleverness.\n- Name things after what they do to streams.\n\n## Development Workflow\n\n- Do not invent abstractions that hide streams.\n- Do not suggest mainstream frameworks unless explicitly asked.\n- Do not optimize prematurely.\n- When uncertain, ask at the architectural level, not the implementation level.\n- Explanations should align with Plan 9 / Datomic / Lisp lineage.\n- Program store writes go through `engine/store-put`; any other store write\n  the store-write audit (`test/yin/vm/store_write_audit_test.clj`)\n  detects must be on its allowlist; its docstring states the residual it\n  leaves to review.\n\n### Problem Decomposition\n\nIf a problem appears complex:\n1. Decompose it into streams.\n2. Identify invariants.\n3. Restrict degrees of freedom.\n4. Make causality explicit.\n5. Let structure emerge from constraints.\n\n## Operational & Specialized Guides\n\n- **Advanced Concepts**: See [`docs/agents/advanced-concepts.md`](../agents/advanced-concepts.md) for parallel transport, capability tokens, and entanglement.\n- **Build & Test**: See [`docs/agents/build-n-test.md`](../agents/build-n-test.md) for build, lint, and test commands and TDD guidelines.\n- **Engineering Team Roster**: See [`docs/agents/team.md`](../agents/team.md) for engineering team roles and LLM model assignments.\n- **Agent Delegation**: See [`docs/agents/roles/orchestrator.md`](../agents/roles/orchestrator.md) for collaboration protocols and delegating tasks or reviews through `agy`, `cmd`, `glm`, `deepseek`, and `codex`; the orchestrator's append-only work log is [`docs/orchestrator-log.md`](../orchestrator-log.md).\n- **Website & Content**: See [`docs/agents/format.md`](../agents/format.md) for `.chp` and `.blog` EDN/Hiccup file specifications.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793032277025-architect-dao-star-demo-scope.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793033722326-architect-dao-star-demo-amend-grid.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793034230245-stream-engineer-dao-star-cart-build.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793032277025-architect-dao-star-demo-scope.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793033722326-architect-dao-star-demo-amend-grid.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1793034230245-stream-engineer-dao-star-cart-build.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"Completed-GMT: 2026-10-26 16:34:14 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-26 23:34:14 +0700\n\nI edited no files. The rulings below rest on these sources: the `dao.space.query` public surface (`relation`, `current`, `q`, `collect`), the signatures of `transact/prepare-tx` and `dao.star.driver`, `jvm.clj`'s function list, and `deps.edn`. I did not open the lambdacart cljs sources, any Dockerfile, the `store.cljc` internals, or the Phase B world fixture in `driver_test.clj`. Where a ruling depends on one of these, it carries a \"verify\" note, collected at the end of section 7.\n\n## 1. dao.space store for the demo\n\n**Ruling:** no file-backed store. The demo builds an in-memory datom vector once at boot and gives `q` a `current` view over a `relation`.\n\n- Boot loads `catalog.edn`, which is plain `tx-data`.\n- It runs `transact/prepare-tx {:base-datoms [] :tx-data tx}`. That answers `{:datoms [...] :tempids ...}` in canonical d5 shape `[e a v t m]`.\n- The catalog db is then `(query/current (query/relation datoms))`.\n- Queries are `(query/collect (query/q '[:find ... :in $ ... :where ...] db arg ...))`. The `$` input is the view value. `q` opens nothing and closes nothing, so there is no handle to own.\n\n**Reasons:**\n- The catalog is read-only for shoppers. The cart lives in a continuation, so nothing in the demo writes.\n- The store layer (`store.cljc`, `store/fs.cljc`) is content-addressed file persistence with locks and a head/version file. That is durability machinery the demo does not need, and it adds a lock and a directory for the container to own.\n- The fixtures for the Datomic conformance corpus feed `query/q` by this same route (relation values), so it is the intended in-process composition for tests.\n\n**Schema and refs.** `prepare-tx` computes the set of `:db.type/ref` attributes from schema datoms in the base or the same tx. So the catalog starts with a schema block:\n- `:item/images` and `:item/tagories` as `{:db/ident ... :db/valueType :db.type/ref :db/cardinality :db.cardinality/many}`;\n- `:store/catalogs` or the minimal tenant link, if kept.\n\nVerify the exact `:db/cardinality` handling against `transact_test.cljc` before the brief fixes it.\n\n**Entity ids.** Do not carry Datomic entity ids. Use tempids in `tx-data` (or nested maps if `parse-tx-data` supports them, which I did not confirm). Items should carry a stable lookup key, `:item/slug` or a string id derived at extraction, so the URL token for item detail does not depend on allocated eids. Resolve refs to nested entity maps in the EDN, not to keywords. Keywords are only right for enum-like values, and tagory names are data, not idents.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- A file-backed `dao.space.store`. It adds locking and a head file for nothing.\n- Keeping Datomic entity ids. They are meaningless outside that database and would collide with `datom/first-user-id` allocation.\n- The compat layer. The owner excluded it.\n\n## 2. Extraction plan\n\n**Extract:**\n- The store entity, with name only.\n- One minimal tenant, if the schema requires the link. Otherwise drop it.\n- All items with `:item/name`, `:item/price`, `:item/description`.\n- Each item's `:item/images` (`:image/url`, `:image/alt`) and `:item/tagories` (`:tagory/name`).\n\nSkip accounts, wallets, orders and addresses, as proposed. They are shopper-private and the cart is a continuation.\n\n**Form:** run once, by the orchestrator or a bb task, on shiva:\n`docker exec lambdacart-app-1 clj -M -e \"<form>\"` with the connection string you gave. The form does a Datomic `pull` over the item eids with the pattern above, converts entity maps to plain nested maps without `:db/id`, and `pr-str`s one EDN vector of item maps to stdout.\n\nTwo cautions:\n- Redirect only stdout, because the JVM may print warnings on stderr.\n- Do not pass the password beyond the already-public compose default. Do not commit connection strings.\n\n**On disk:** `resources/dao_star_cart/catalog.edn` in the demo. It is a vector of item maps plus the store map, and boot turns it into `tx-data`. Check the EDN into the demo worktree: it is a few hundred small maps, plain data and reproducible from the extraction form. Record the extraction form and date in a header comment on the file.\n\n**Images:** copy only the files the catalog's `:image/url` values name, from `/Users/sto/workspace/lambdacart/public/images`, into `resources/dao_star_cart/public/images/`. Fall back to the full directory only if it is small. Check the total size first. If it is large, mount at run time and keep the images out of git. Copy the css and font files the theme needs into `public/css/`.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- Extracting at app boot against live Datomic. The demo must not depend on the live stack.\n- Hand-writing a catalog. That would not reproduce the live data.\n\n## 3. Static assets vs the boundary\n\n**Confirmed, with one tightening.** Host isolation is the correct reading. The boundary is application-agnostic and the composition's host edge owns dispatch.\n\nThe host edge is one http-kit handler. For each request:\n- A path under `/images/` or `/css/` is served from disk and never enters the boundary.\n- Every other path goes to `http/accept!`.\n\n**Tightening:** the static branch must resolve the path against the asset root and refuse anything that normalizes outside it (`..`, encoded separators, absolute paths). Serve only regular files, set `Content-Type` from the extension, and answer 404 otherwise. This is the only new attack surface the demo adds, and it needs a test.\n\nDo not route static requests through the boundary \"for uniformity\". Exchanges are leased and ingress is a bounded window, and image traffic would evict real requests.\n\n## 4. The app\n\n**Layout:** `src/cljc/dao/star/cart/` is wrong, because it implies the library layer owns the app. Following the repo, which has no existing `demo` source tree (`yin.demo` is a main namespace with a `bb demo` task), use:\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/` for host code: `main.clj` (boot, composition, listener, static edge), `catalog.clj` (load and query).\n- `src/cljc/dao/star_cart/` for pure code: `program.cljc` (the session program source), `view.cljc` (projection and HTML).\n- `resources/dao_star_cart/` for data and assets.\n- `bb.edn` task `cart` running `clj -M -m dao.star-cart.main` on port 3004, matching the `demo` task shape. Verify that namespace-to-path munging in the brief.\n\n**The session program.** A yin.vm program run through `dao.await/run`, which the driver already steps. Per Phase B's ruling, the program is pure sequential logic. It reads inputs from its input medium with `<!`, holds `cart` as a local in its loop, and emits a model to its view medium with `>!`. The model is a plain map: the page (`:catalog`, `:item`, `:cart`, `:checkout`), the item key when on a detail page, and the cart (a vector of `{:item key :qty n}`).\n\nInput kinds are fixed by what the signals carry: navigate, add, remove and checkout, each a small map decoded by `dao.star.signals/decode`. The cart is never a server table. It is a local binding in the continuation, so it survives exactly as long as the lineage lease does.\n\nThe program does no catalog queries. The driver-side view function does, because it is a pure projection: `(project-fn occurrence-id model)` calls `catalog/items` and `catalog/item` over the db value closed over at composition time. That keeps the program free of host values and keeps re-projection (subscribe and stale) valid without stepping.\n\n**Projection.** `view.cljc` renders the SSE text using Datastar's `datastar-patch-elements` format. It has one function per page, plus the begin function for the complete HTML document at `/` (`datastar.js`, the stylesheet link, actions carrying the root id, `data-init=\"@get('/s/<token>')\"`). Port the live site's CSS from `public/css` and take the grid structure from `src/cljs/lambdacart/grid.cljs` and `main.cljs`. I haven't read those; the implementer reads them first and reproduces markup structure, not behavior.\n\nServe `datastar.js` from the static edge. Vendor the file into `public/js/`, don't hotlink a CDN. Pin the version, and make sure the SSE event names match that version's vocabulary.\n\n**Composition (per merged Mode 1):** boundary (`http/make` or the equivalent from Phase A), the exchange judge with `close-exchange` and `open-exchanges`, the lineage judge, the router over the session-facts medium and the lineage lease, and the driver. `session-facts` is a `memory-log` declared `:retention :complete`. Subscription exchanges carry the required `:dao.lease/max`. One tick loop drives the judges, router and driver at the cadence the lease sizing rules require.\n\nCopy the wiring from the Phase B test world in `test/dao/star/driver_test.clj` rather than inventing it. That fixture is the proven composition. I did not read it, so verify the helper names in the brief.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- The cart as a dao.space table. It contradicts the demo's point.\n- Catalog queries inside the program. They would put host state into a continuation.\n- A new namespace under `dao.star.*`.\n\n## 5. Deployment\n\n**Image:** the demo image needs the repo's source and a trimmed dependency set. Two findings from `deps.edn`:\n- The root `deps.edn` carries `com.datomic/peer`, `org.postgresql`, `stigmergy/chp 0.1.0-SNAPSHOT` and `io.github.rctcwyvrn/blake3`. `stigmergy/chp` exists under `~/.m2/repository/stigmergy`, which suggests a local install that a clean Docker build can't resolve. I did not confirm how it resolves elsewhere.\n- Root `:paths` includes build output directories that don't exist in a clean checkout, such as `build/antlr/*/classes`.\n\n**Ruling:** a demo-specific `deps.edn` at `docker/dao-star-cart/deps.edn` with only what the demo uses: `http-kit`, `core.async`, `data.json`, the blake3 dependency, and the `:paths` list `[\"src/clj\" \"src/cljc\" \"resources\"]`. The Dockerfile copies `src/clj` and `src/cljc` from the repo plus the demo's resources. This avoids Datomic, Postgres and chp entirely. Verify by loading the demo's namespaces under that trimmed alias: if `dao.stream.*`, `dao.space.*` or `yin.vm.*` pull in a namespace that needs a dropped dependency, add that one dependency back. That check is the implementer's first task.\n\n**Build and run:**\n- Stage 1 (build): `clojure:temurin-21-tools-deps` or the image the lambdacart app already uses. Copy deps first and run `clj -P` to prime the cache.\n- Final stage runs `clj -M -m dao.star-cart.main` with `PORT=3004` and the asset root as environment.\n- No AOT or uberjar for the first cut. Source-loading start time is acceptable for a demo. An uberjar is a later optimization.\n- Bake images and css into the image because they are small and make the image self-contained. If the image size from section 2 says otherwise, mount `./public` read-only instead.\n\n**Port:** 3004 in the container, mapped `3004:3004` in a separate compose file or `docker run`. Do not edit the lambdacart compose project.\n\n**Shiva contention:** the build runs plain `clj -P` and a JVM, which is light. It must not run `bb` tasks that touch the antlr or flutter lanes and must not run the repo's full test suites on shiva. Run the tests locally, build the image on shiva with `docker build` only, use `--memory`/`--cpus` limits on the container, and schedule the build when the orchestrator knows those lanes are quiet. Build with BuildKit and one parallel job.\n\n## 6. Test plan (JVM only)\n\n| Acceptance | Test | Medium |\n|---|---|---|\n| Catalog renders N real items with real image URLs from dao.space | Boot from `catalog.edn`. Count items by `q`, request `/`, then the subscribe, and assert N `<img src=\"/images/...\">` elements, each URL equal to a catalog `:image/url`. | loopback socket |\n| Image URLs resolve | For every URL in the first page, GET it from the static edge and assert 200, the right content type and non-empty bytes. | loopback |\n| Add-to-cart parks and resumes with state intact | POST an add for item A, then one for B, and assert the cart view shows both with the right quantities. A third add of A shows qty 2, which proves state persists in the continuation across resumes. | loopback |\n| Cart contents render | Navigate to the cart page and assert names, prices, quantities and a computed total. | in-memory driver world |\n| Stale token is harmless | Replay the pre-add token and assert the cart does not change. | in-memory |\n| Static path traversal refused | GET `/images/../deps.edn`, `/images/%2e%2e/...` and an absolute path, and assert 404 with no file content. | loopback |\n| Catalog query path | A direct test that `catalog/items` goes through `dao.space.query/q` and `collect`, with a fixture of 3 items. | pure |\n| Boot from the checked-in file | Parse `catalog.edn`, transact, and assert every item has a name, a price and at least one image. | pure |\n\nKeep one full-page loopback test. Mark any test that needs real time `^:slow`. Everything else goes in the quick tier. Add no CLJS or CLJD lane work.\n\n## 7. File ownership and risks\n\n**The implementer owns (new files only):**\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/*.clj`\n- `src/cljc/dao/star_cart/*.cljc`\n- `resources/dao_star_cart/**`\n- `docker/dao-star-cart/**`\n- `test/dao/star_cart/**`\n- one `bb.edn` entry (`cart`)\n\n**Not touched:** `dao.star.*`, `dao.stream.*`, `dao.space.*`, `dao.lease`, `yin.vm.*`, `dao.await`, and the root `deps.edn`. Changes there come back to the Architect.\n\n**Risks, in order:**\n1. **The Datastar contract.** Event names, the data prefix per line and the required blank line differ by version. Pin the version and test the exact bytes against the vendored `datastar.js`'s own expectations. A `text/event-stream` response with the wrong framing fails silently in the browser.\n2. **Trimmed deps hiding a transitive require.** See section 5. The implementer finds out in minutes by loading the namespaces under the trimmed alias.\n3. **Subscription max reconnects.** Every healthy subscription reconnects once per `:dao.lease/max` (Mode 1's cost). Pick a max long enough to be invisible in a demo, and test that the reconnect re-projects the current cart.\n4. **The program/driver division of labor.** If the implementer lets the program touch the catalog or egress, the cart stops being a clean continuation and fact-before-UI ordering breaks. The brief says the program only reads inputs and emits a model.\n5. **Image licensing and size.** Product photos are the shop's. The demo is on a private shiva port. The brief should say nothing is published or committed to a public remote without the owner's say-so. I'd keep `images/` out of git if the set is large.\n6. **Extraction hygiene.** The Datomic password in the proposed connection string is the compose default. It must not land in a committed file.\n7. **Price units.** `2400` looks like minor units, but I didn't verify. Check how `grid.cljs` formats it before rendering.\n8. **Tick loop and the JVM.** One thread drives the judges, router and driver. Its cadence must satisfy the lease sizing relations, and the demo must set it from `lease/renewal-interval`, not by hand.\n\n**To verify before the brief is final:** the `prepare-tx` shape for nested maps and cardinality-many refs (`transact_test.cljc`); the Phase B world fixture's helper names (`driver_test.clj`); the lambdacart `grid.cljs` and `main.cljs` markup; the size of `public/images`; and the trimmed-deps load.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-26 16:58:25 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-26 23:58:25 +0700\n\nI edited nothing. This amendment rests on `router.cljc` (`classify`, `make-router`), `driver.cljc` (`make-driver`), `jvm.clj` (`handler`, `listen!`), and `git status` in the demo worktree. I did not read `grid.chp`, the lambdacart editor code, `store.cljc`, or `transact_test.cljc`. The editor's feature list below comes from your prompt.\n\n## Two facts that shape the rulings\n\n1. **`router/classify` is closed.** It is hardwired to `/`, `/s/<t>` and `/k/<t>`, and any other path is `:unrouted`. `make-router` takes one `:begin` medium, and `make-driver` takes one `:program`, `:project-fn` and `:begin-fn`. A composition is therefore one app. The Mode 1 layer is merged and signed off, so it should not grow a \"mode\" for this demo.\n2. **`jvm/handler` is private, and `listen!` binds one listener per boundary.** The demo's host edge needs to serve static files and dispatch to two boundaries on one port, so it needs the accept path. This is the one change I rule for a Phase A file; see ruling 2.\n\n## 1. Mutable store\n\n**Ruling:** option (a), plus one small persistence step. The store is an atom holding the accumulated datom vector. Each edit runs `transact/prepare-tx` against the current base datoms and swaps in the result. The storefront and the editor query `(query/current (query/relation @datoms))` at request time. On every successful save the demo also writes `catalog.edn` back, atomically (write a temp file beside it, then rename).\n\n**Persisted form:** the file stays plain item maps, which is the format already extracted, not raw datoms. The save path derives the maps from the db by `q` or `pull` and writes the whole vector. Boot loads that file. Edits therefore survive a restart, and the file stays human-readable and re-extractable.\n\n**Why:**\n- The demo's honesty requirement is that edits don't vanish on restart. A snapshot file delivers that with about ten lines of host code.\n- `dao.space.store` is content-addressed persistence with a lock and head file. A 249-item catalog gains nothing from it, and it would make the demo container own a lock directory.\n- The cost is an O(n) rewrite of a 76K file per save. That is trivial here.\n\n**Concurrency:** the atom serializes writes. A save is `swap!` with a function that calls `prepare-tx` on the current value, so two concurrent editors cannot lose each other's updates.\n\n**Where the file lives:** the image bakes the seed `catalog.edn`. The save target must be a path outside the image's read-only layer, a mounted volume, set by an environment variable (`CATALOG_PATH`). On first boot with an empty volume the demo copies the seed there. State this in the Dockerfile notes. If the path is unwritable, the demo logs it and keeps editing in memory. It does not crash.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- (b) A file-backed `dao.space.store`: locking machinery for no gain.\n- (c) Periodic snapshots: an edit made just before a crash is lost, and a timer adds a clock thread. Snapshot on save is simpler.\n- Persisting raw datoms: it loses the human-readable seed and the re-extraction path.\n\n## 2. The editor session\n\n**Ruling:** a second, complete composition, not a new router kind.\n\n- The demo defines one `assemble` function that wires boundary, exchange judge, lineage judge, router, driver, media and ticks. It calls it twice: once for the shop and once for the grid, each with its own program, `project-fn`, `begin-fn` and media.\n- The shop composition is as scoped earlier.\n- The grid composition has its own boundary instance. Its router and driver are unchanged `dao.star` code, so its own `/`, `/s/<t>` and `/k/<t>` are the grid's begin, subscribe and input.\n- One tick loop drives both compositions.\n\n**How `/grid` fits:** the host edge, which already owns dispatch, strips the prefix. A request for `/grid` or `/grid/...` is passed to the grid boundary with `:uri` rewritten (`/grid` becomes `/`, `/grid/s/x` becomes `/s/x`), and everything else goes to the shop boundary. The grid's `begin-fn` and `project-fn` emit URLs with the `/grid` prefix, so the browser's actions come back through the same edge. Both faces are one site on one port. Static paths are handled before either boundary, as ruled earlier.\n\n**Edge handler.** The demo edge is its own http-kit handler. Because `jvm/handler` is private, make the smallest change: change `(defn- handler` to `(defn handler` in `src/clj/dao/stream/http/jvm.clj`, with a one-line docstring. The demo edge calls `(jvm/handler boundary)` for each boundary and dispatches to the right one. No logic changes. This is the only edit to a merged file. If the owner prefers zero touches to merged code, the alternative is a copy of the 25-line handler in the demo, which duplicates `read-body` and `socket`. I recommend the one-word change.\n\n**Rejected:**\n- A new `/grid` kind in `router/classify`. It would change a signed-off library for one demo, and the router has one `:begin` medium and the driver one program, so a kind would still need a second driver.\n- Begin-with-a-mode. One program would have to be both apps, and the program-only-reads-inputs boundary would blur.\n- A second port. The owner wants one site.\n\n**The editor program.** It is a yin.vm continuation under `dao.await`, exactly like the cart program: it reads inputs from its input medium and emits a model to its view medium. Its state in the continuation is only UI state: the selected item key, the edit draft (field values typed so far), and a filter string. The catalog itself is not in the continuation.\n\nInputs: select item, set field (name, price, description), add image URL or remove image, add tagory or remove tagory, save, cancel. `signals.cljc` decodes the typed fields from the Datastar signals.\n\n**Where the transact happens: the driver, as you proposed.** The program must not do host I/O. When the user hits save, the program emits a model that includes the pending edit `{:edit {:item key :changes {...}}}`. The `project-fn` is pure, so it cannot do the transact either. The side effect needs a defined home, because the driver is library code that I'm not changing.\n\n**Ruling:** an `:apply-fn` the composition supplies. I have not verified `dao.star.driver` has such a hook. `make-driver` as read takes `:program :project-fn :begin-fn` and no effect hook. So there are two options:\n\n- **(i) Intercept in the host before the driver (my recommendation).** Add one thin interpreter, `grid-effects`, in the demo: a stepped function reading the grid program's *view medium* with its own cursor, which is a peer observer of the same medium the driver reads. When it sees a model carrying `:edit`, it runs the transact and bumps a `:version` in a small `edits` atom. The `project-fn` reads the db fresh and so shows the saved result. This preserves the division: the program emits data, one host interpreter performs the I/O, and the driver is untouched.\n- (ii) Add an effect hook to `dao.star.driver`. This changes the merged library and needs its own review.\n\nTake (i). One consequence to pin: the transact must complete before the projection that reports \"saved\". That ordering is not given by the driver. To get it, the program does not claim success. It emits the edit and the projection renders \"saving\". The effects interpreter, after the transact, appends a confirmation input (`{:saved item-key}`) back to the session's input medium, and the program's next model shows \"saved\". This costs one extra round trip and keeps fact-before-UI intact. Validation errors (a negative price, an empty name) take the same path: the interpreter appends `{:rejected reason}` instead.\n\n**Validation belongs in the effects interpreter, not in the program or the browser.** Signals are the client's unverified claim, so price must be an integer in range, name non-empty and bounded, and image URLs must be restricted to `/images/` paths already on disk or http(s) URLs per the owner's choice. State the bounds in the brief.\n\n## 3. Cross-face coherence\n\n**Ruling:** accept it honestly and say so; no broadcast.\n\n- A storefront subscription renders the catalog as of its last projection. A grid save is visible to a shopper on their next navigation or reload, because each navigation runs a fresh `q`.\n- A one-line note in the shop footer says \"catalog as of last page load\", and the demo README states it.\n- The editor itself shows its own save immediately, since its own flow includes the confirmation round trip.\n\n**Why:** a broadcast would need the save to cause every live lineage's driver to re-project. The driver re-projects on session events, not on catalog changes, and Mode 1 deliberately has no shared-state wakeups. Faking it with a ticker that injects synthetic inputs into every session would make `dao.star` look like a pub-sub system, which it is not. For a demo, the stale-until-navigation behavior is also a correct illustration of the design: the UI is a derivation, and derivations re-run when asked.\n\n**If the owner later wants live update,** the honest route is that the shop's `project-fn` closes over a `version` counter, and a shop tick-driven interpreter appends a \"refresh\" input to sessions whose last projection used an older version. That is a follow-up, not part of this demo.\n\n## 4. File ownership, tests, auth\n\n**Ownership deltas** (new files, in addition to the earlier list):\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/store.clj`: the mutable db atom, `transact-edit!`, snapshot-on-save with atomic rename, boot loading.\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/edge.clj`: the http-kit dispatch (static, `/grid`, shop).\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/assemble.clj`: the `assemble` function called twice.\n- `src/clj/dao/star_cart/grid_effects.clj`: the effects interpreter.\n- `src/cljc/dao/star_cart/grid_program.cljc` and `grid_view.cljc`: the editor program and its pure view.\n- One edit to `src/clj/dao/stream/http/jvm.clj`: `defn-` becomes `defn` on `handler`. Architect-approved, by this document.\n\nThe UI is the implementer's own minimal dark theme (one small stylesheet in `public/css/`). Reproducing lambdacart's markup or css is out of scope, as the owner said. The earlier instruction to read `grid.cljs` and port css is withdrawn.\n\n**Images stay out of git. Confirmed.** `git status` shows the 228 images untracked with no ignore rule covering them. Add a `.gitignore` line, or a `.git/info/exclude` entry, for `resources/dao_star_cart/public/images/`, so no one stages 68 MB by accident. The Dockerfile copies them from the build context, so the build context on shiva needs them. The orchestrator copies the directory there out of band. `catalog.edn` itself is checked in. It is the saved state, so tell the user that edits in a running container do not touch the repo copy.\n\n**Auth: none for the demo.** The owner said users can edit. I rule no authentication, and the brief must say so plainly:\n- The grid is open to anyone who can reach port 3004.\n- Mitigations that cost nothing: bind the container to the shiva interface the owner already uses, keep it off any public route, and validate every field as in ruling 2.\n- The token gate is not authorization. It identifies a session. ShiBi is the later answer, per the earlier rulings.\n- The README states \"no authentication; do not expose.\"\n\n**Test plan deltas (JVM):**\n\n| Acceptance | Test | Medium |\n|---|---|---|\n| Grid renders items | GET `/grid`, subscribe, and assert N item rows with names and prices from the db | loopback |\n| Edit save transacts and the storefront's next query sees it | Edit item A's price through the grid, wait for the confirmation model, then request the shop catalog and assert the new price | loopback |\n| Validation rejects bad input | Negative price, empty name, oversized description, and an image path outside the allowed set each yield `:rejected` and an unchanged db | in-memory |\n| Tagory add and remove | Add a tagory to an item and assert a `q` on the tagory finds it, then remove it and assert it is gone. Include a multi-tagory item. | in-memory |\n| Image add and remove | Same pattern on `:item/images` | in-memory |\n| Persistence | Save an edit, build a fresh store from the written file, and assert the edit is present. Also assert the written file parses as the same item maps. | pure, temp dir |\n| Atomic write | A save that fails mid-write (inject a failing writer) leaves the old file intact | pure |\n| Concurrent saves | Two interleaved saves to different items both survive | in-memory |\n| Path rewrite | `/grid/s/x` reaches the grid boundary as `/s/x`, and `/grid` does not reach the shop boundary | edge unit |\n| Static traversal still refused | As before, now also under `/grid/images/..` | loopback |\n| Stale shop view | After a grid save, an open shop subscription still shows the old price until navigation, then shows the new one. This pins the ruled behavior. | in-memory |\n\n**Risks added:**\n1. **Effects interpreter ordering.** The \"saved\" model must follow the transact. The brief pins the confirmation round trip and tests it.\n2. **Two compositions on one tick loop.** A slow grid save must not starve the shop's judges. Keep the transact synchronous and small, which it is at this size, and run both compositions' steps in one loop.\n3. **Duplicate wiring drift.** `assemble` must be one function with the program, projection and prefix as parameters. Copy-pasting the composition invites the two to diverge.\n4. **Rewritten paths and URLs.** If the grid's projection forgets the `/grid` prefix on an action, the request lands on the shop boundary. A test that scans the rendered grid HTML for `@get`/`@post` URLs and asserts each starts with `/grid/` catches it.\n5. **Catalog file write on a read-only mount.** Handled by logging and continuing in memory, with a test.\n\n**To verify before the brief is final:** whether `prepare-tx` supports retracting and replacing a cardinality-many ref (tagory remove) the way the editor needs, which I did not check; how `dao.await` programs receive a late-appended input (the confirmation) while parked, which follows the Phase B pattern but I did not re-read it; and that the effects interpreter's extra cursor on the view medium is allowed by the driver's single-reader assumption. If the view medium is a ring buffer the driver also reads, a second reader with its own cursor is safe under the stream contract. If not, give the effects interpreter its own tee.\nCompleted-GMT: 2026-10-27 (clock not read; no precise timestamp available)\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-27, +07 (clock not read)\n\nThe cart demo is built and its tests pass. Three things are not done: `datastar.js` is not vendored, the smoke run was not done as a standalone process, and the Docker image was not built. I did not write the findings report to `collab/`; the report is below.\n\n## Checks\n- `clojure -M:test -n dao.star-cart.catalog-test -n dao.star-cart.view-test -n dao.star-cart.edge-test -n dao.star-cart.app-test`: 28 tests, 478 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors. This includes the `^:slow` test that fetches all 228 image URLs.\n- `clj -M:kondo --lint` on `src/clj/dao/star_cart`, `src/cljc/dao/star_cart`, `test/dao/star_cart` and `jvm.clj`: 0 errors, 0 warnings.\n- I did not re-run the `dao.star` or `dao.stream.http` suites. I changed none of their sources.\n\n## Smoke evidence\n- `clj -M -m dao.star-cart.main` and `curl` were refused by the permission gate, so I could not do the pasted self-check.\n- The nearest evidence is `app_test.clj`. Each test boots the real `main/start!` path on an ephemeral port: catalog from `catalog.edn`, shop and grid compositions, edge, tick loop and listener.\n- Over raw sockets it shows:\n  - the shop page and 247 image tags in the subscription;\n  - the grid listing 249 items;\n  - a grid price edit written to the catalog file and seen by the shop after navigation;\n  - traversal attempts answered 404.\n\n## Trimmed deps\n- **Evidence:** I loaded `dao.star-cart.main` and inspected which namespaces reference `datomic`, `taoensso`, `stigmergy`, `io.aviso` or `ring`. Only `user` (JVM startup noise from the chp jar) references `stigmergy.ikota`, and http-kit itself references `ring.websocket.protocols`. The demo's own closure touches none of the dropped libraries.\n- **What I couldn't do:** I could not run `clojure` from a copy of the trimmed `deps.edn`, because `cd`, symlinks and `sh` were refused. So it is not verified under the trimmed classpath itself. I added `ring-websocket-protocols` 1.14.1 explicitly because http-kit needs it.\n\n## What was built\n- **`src/cljc/dao/star_cart/`:**\n  - `program.cljc` and `grid_program.cljc`: the two yin.vm programs. They use only `assoc`, `get`, `if`, `+`, `-` and `def`, because the VM has no `dissoc`, `cond` or `str`. The cart is a slug-to-quantity map, and a removal decrements while the view hides zero lines.\n  - `view.cljc`: the pure shop view and shared helpers. Its SSE bytes are tested against Datastar 1.0.0-RC.7's vocabulary from my knowledge of that version, not against the vendored file.\n  - `grid_view.cljc`: the grid projection. A test scans every `@get`/`@post` URL for the `/grid` prefix.\n- **`src/clj/dao/star_cart/`:**\n  - `store.clj`: the catalog store, with edits transacted in a `swap!` and each save written by temp file plus atomic rename.\n  - `catalog.clj`: reads through `q` and `collect`.\n  - `assemble.clj`: the one wiring function, called for shop and grid, with renewal intervals from `lease/renewal-interval`.\n  - `edge.clj`: static serving with traversal protection, and the `/grid` prefix rewrite.\n  - `grid_effects.clj`: validation, the transact, and the `{:saved ..}` / `{:rejected ..}` confirmation.\n  - `main.clj`: boot, one tick loop, listener on port 3004.\n- **Docker, `bb`, docs:**\n  - `docker/dao-star-cart/{deps.edn,Dockerfile,README.md}`.\n  - The `cart` task in `bb.edn`.\n  - `resources/dao_star_cart/README.md`, `public/css/cart.css`, and `vendor-datastar.sh`.\n  - The header comment on `catalog.edn`.\n- **Tests:** they cover the combined table, including the full-page loopback test and the stale-shop-view test. The `prepare-tx` and `q` facts I relied on are in the next section.\n\n## Findings\n1. **`datastar.js` is not vendored.** I could not fetch it: there was no local copy and network access needs approval. `resources/dao_star_cart/vendor-datastar.sh` fetches the pinned RC.7 bundle and prints its checksum. Run it once (with network) before pages work in a browser. The browser has not been run against any of this.\n2. **Two empty placeholder items** (`item-76`, `item-105`, no name, price or image) are in the source data. The shop hides them and the grid lists them, so you can fix them there. The boot test asserts every non-blank item has a price and an image.\n3. **`prepare-tx` limits.** Nested entity maps are not supported, so the store flattens items into item, image and tagory entities linked by tempids. A card-one value re-asserted unchanged produces conflicting rows and breaks `q`, so the edit path skips unchanged fields.\n4. **Retracts need metadata.** A bare `[:db/retract e a v]` is treated as an add, so retracts carry `{:db/op :db/retract}`. Removing a card-many ref works, and the tagory and image tests prove it.\n5. **Grid confirmation is a synthetic routed input.** After a transact, the effects interpreter appends a `:kind :input` message with a null exchange to the session's routed medium. The driver then steps the program and projects \"saved\". Each confirmation also writes one effect for a null exchange to the egress medium, which the boundary ignores.\n6. **Images are not ignored by git.** The brief says an ignore rule is already in `.gitignore`, but `git status` lists all 228 images as untracked and `.gitignore` has no `dao_star_cart` line. I did not edit `.gitignore`. Add an ignore line (or a `.git/info/exclude` entry) before anyone stages.\n7. **Shop catalog uses a fixed `max-open` of 256 exchanges and a one-hour subscription cap.** Both are defaults in `assemble.clj` and easy to change.\n8. **The `kondo` \"handler is private\" error** is a stale cache. `jvm.clj` has `defn handler`, and linting `jvm.clj` together with the demo files clears it.\n9. **Not tested:**\n   - a browser;\n   - the Docker build;\n   - the one-hour subscription cap and reconnect, beyond the existing Mode 1 tests;\n   - a catalog on a read-only mount other than the nonexistent-directory case.\n10. **Left behind:** no probe or temp files remain. I did not stage, commit or touch Docker. The only non-demo edits are the `cart` entry in `bb.edn`, and `jvm.clj` was already changed by the orchestrator.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
