uerySelectorAll(\"script\"))st.add(e);var jt=e=>{let t=e instanceof HTMLScriptElement?[e]:e.querySelectorAll(\"script\");for(let n of t)if(!st.has(n)){let r=document.createElement(\"script\");for(let{name:s,value:o}of n.attributes)r.setAttribute(s,o);r.text=n.text,n.replaceWith(r),st.add(r)}},Vt=(e,t,n)=>{for(let r of e){let s=t.cloneNode(!0);jt(s),r[n](s)}},$t=(e,t,n)=>{\n---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Subsystem-first tests: `bb test:sub`\n\n`bb test:sub <subsystem>...` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the tests belonging to\none or more canonical subsystems (defined in `src/dev/subsystems.edn`). Selection\nis by longest namespace prefix matching.\n\n```sh\nbb test:sub <subsystem>...        # All three lanes in parallel (fast form)\nbb test:sub:clj <subsystem>...    # JVM lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljs <subsystem>...   # Node lane alone\nbb test:sub:cljd <subsystem>...   # Dart lane alone\nbb test:sub:list <subsystem>...   # Show namespaces, builds, Class B seams & dependents; runs nothing\nbb test:sub <subsystem>... --slow # Include slow tests (-i :slow on JVM, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1 on Node/Dart)\nbb -cp src/dev -m affected-test   # Run affected-test self-tests directly in Babashka\n```\n\n92:**The open exchange is leased.** The judge runs inside the peer that holds the HTTP boundary and is composed with the boundary's `close-exchange` as reclaim procedure and `open-exchanges` as its inventory (`dao.stream.http.md`, Inventory). For each `:http/request` it observes it grants unsolicited on a lease medium it writes: subject `{:http/exchange x}`, holder the egress medium's identity, a duration in `{:ms 1 :s 1000}`, and a `:dao.lease/max` cap that subscription exchanges are required to carry, so no subscription is uncapped (architect ruling 2026-10-24, `collab/1792841840885-architect-dao-star-m1-reconcile-r2`). Reclaim closes the exchange; the adapter deposits `:http/closed`, then the judge records `:lapsed`. A responder that keeps an exchange open observes its grant and appends `{:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/renewal :dao.lease/lease L}` to the egress medium at under half the duration, from its own control flow, against its own tick stream. The judge and `egress-step` are peer observers of that medium; each ignores the other's facts. A one-shot responder answers inside the granted duration and renews nothing. An exchange nobody answers lapses by `:silence`; Datastar's reconnect opens a new one.\n94:**A gap is loss, and nothing is forwarded about it.** The judge, on an ingress `gap`, reconciles both ways against the inventory: an open exchange without a lease is granted afresh, and a leased exchange absent from the inventory is recorded `:lapsed` with cause `:policy`, so nothing is held unleased and nothing closed stays leased (inventory reconciliation; `dao.lease.md`'s reclaim-and-regrant is reserved for a lost ledger). The router forwards nothing about a gap: a request or terminal event lost in the evicted window is unrouted, and a forward whose `append!` answers anything but `ok` is the same loss; an unrouted request's exchange lapses by `:silence`. Drivers learn that an exchange is gone from the lease medium they already read for their grants: a `:lapsed` record for one of their exchanges drops it (`dao.lease.md`, Carriage). A driver whose cursor on any medium it reads answers `gap` forgets every subscription it holds and renews none; they lapse by `:silence` and close, and Datastar's reconnect subscribes again. Session media are declared at assembly like any deposit destination, evict-oldest with a capacity, and sized against the driver's lag.\n96:**Keep-alive is the responder's.** On its tick it may append an `:http/chunk` carrying an SSE comment line. That chunk is bytes for the wire, so a host that reports write failure discovers a dead peer by writing; the renewal is evidence for the judge. Neither stands in for the other. A peer's close or a reported write failure is deposited as `:http/closed`; the responder reads it as data and drops the exchange, and the judge ends the exchange lease with cause `:policy`. A host whose send buffers and whose close notification is silent for an idle exchange (http-kit is one) reports neither, so a chunk written to a dead peer is not evidence of anything, and a responder that keeps renewing a subscription it believes open keeps the lease alive. Such an exchange ends at its lease's `:dao.lease/max`, not before (architect ruling 2026-10-24, `collab/1792841840885-architect-dao-star-m1-reconcile-r2`).\n98:**The lineage is leased** (owner ruling 2026-10-07; the holder choice below is the architect's). A lineage nobody returns to is reclaimed through `dao.lease.md`, not by a timer. The peer holding the parked record is the grantor. When a lineage begins, its judge grants unsolicited on the lease medium: subject and holder `{:dao.star/lineage S}`, duration the composition's idle allowance, `:dao.lease/max` optional. Attribution is by envelope key: the session-facts medium is written only by session drivers, each stamping `:dao.star/lineage` on its facts, renewals and release, and the judge's resolver reads that key; until ShiBi, any writer of that medium can renew, the same exposure the exchange lease has. The driver observes its grant before consuming or projecting, renews at under half the duration from its own control flow, on each consumed input and on its tick while it holds an open subscription, and **stops at its bound**: past it, it consumes nothing and projects nothing, and an input arriving then is handed to the root program as begin. So a connected session is retained for as long as it stays connected, by design, bounded only by the cap; a disconnected one lapses after the idle allowance. The lease ends with the lineage: released by the driver on halt, lapsed by the judge on silence. Reclaim retires the runnable resource, the current parked record; the `:lapsed` record on the lease medium is the judge's own and is what ends the lineage for the router. History, successor facts and lease records are kept (`dao.lease.md`: a reclaim frees the resource, never the record). The subject is distinct from Mode 2's custody subject `{:yin.k/occurrence O}` on the arbitration medium. Leases are process-scoped until the composition settles the three durable duties `dao.lease.md` names.\n100:Sizing: duration exceeds twice the renewal interval; the keep-alive interval stays under the shortest proxy or NAT idle timeout on the path; the ingress window exceeds router and judge lag and each session medium's window exceeds its driver's; a boundary restart loses sockets and ledger together, so its exchange inventory to reclaim is empty. The duration bounds silence, not death. The detection bound for a peer that disconnects while an exchange is idle is the exchange lease's `:dao.lease/max` on a host that does not report the close, and the host's own close notification, when it gives one, on a host that does. The composition sizes `:dao.lease/max` against the longest it will tolerate a dead subscription holding a socket and a driver slot, and accepts that every healthy subscription reconnects at that interval. A false lapse is possible (`dao.lease.md`, Limits).\n164:1. **Thin Client**: A `yin.vm` agent receives a Datastar input through the HTTP boundary's ingress stream, answers with `:http/head`, SSE-formatted `:http/chunk`s and `:http/end` on the egress stream, parks, and resumes on the next input for its current token. Also: a request with no token begins a lineage; a subscribe consumes nothing and a re-subscribe after several inputs receives the current projection; a stale token is re-projected; a closed socket is observed as `:http/closed`; a silent exchange lapses and closes; a lineage nobody returns to lapses, its parked record is retired and its facts remain; a request naming a just-published token is never answered as begin; after an ingress gap reconciliation leaves no exchange unleased and no closed exchange leased, and a driver stops renewing an exchange once it observes the lapse or its own gap; a renewal in flight may still count while the lease is live, and one observed after retirement does not revive it.\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.content\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/content.cljc:25:  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.cbor\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc:53:  (:require\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.dht\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/dht.cljc:28:  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.mem\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/mem.cljc:27:  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing.cljc:27:  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.file\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/file.cljc:40:  (:require #?@(:cljd [[\"dart:convert\" :as convert] [\"dart:io\" :as dart-io]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.coordinate\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/coordinate.cljc:5:  (:require [dao.jing.content.step :as step]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:1:(ns dao.jing.stream\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:24:  (:require [dao.jing :as jing]\nsrc/cljc/dao/jing/stream.cljc:28:  #?@(:cljd [(:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])]))\nsrc/cljc/dao/await.cljc:1:(ns dao.await\nsrc/cljc/dao/await.cljc:36:  (:require [dao.stream :as ds]\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:1:(ns yin.vm\nsrc/cljc/yin/vm.cljc:26:  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:1:(ns dao.space.index\nsrc/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:43:  (:require [dao.data.btree :as bt]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,80p' src/cljc/dao/await.cljc && sed -n '1,100p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc && sed -n '1,85p' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc && sed -n '20,65p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc && sed -n '45,100p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc && rg -n 'blake3|:require|:import' src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc src/clj/dao/host/* 2>/dev/null && ls -la docker/dao-star-cart && nl -ba docker/dao-star-cart/README.md && nl -ba resources/dao_star_cart/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_24","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,80p' src/cljc/dao/await.cljc && sed -n '1,100p' src/cljc/yin/vm.cljc && sed -n '1,85p' src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc && sed -n '20,65p' src/cljc/dao/jing.cljc && sed -n '45,100p' src/cljc/dao/jing/cbor.cljc && rg -n 'blake3|:require|:import' src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc src/clj/dao/host/* 2>/dev/null && ls -la docker/dao-star-cart && nl -ba docker/dao-star-cart/README.md && nl -ba resources/dao_star_cart/README.md\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.await\n  \"Async-style syntax for sequential stream programs that run entirely on\n   yin.vm and dao.stream.\n\n   See docs/design/dao.await.md for the full design. This is v1's surface,\n   ported; what changed is everything underneath it:\n\n   - The module registry is a value. v1 registered 'await and 'dao.await\n     into a global at go* time; here `registry` builds the same bindings as\n     an ordinary yin.vm.module registry value that `run` supplies to the\n     VM. Nothing registers itself at load time.\n   - Resumption is polling. v1's `resume` spliced the :woke vector a v1\n     append returned into the ready queue. No v2 transport is waitable and\n     v2 appends wake nothing, so `resume` simply re-runs the VM: the\n     scheduler polls each parked entry against its transport and continues\n     the continuations the poll resolves.\n   - Cursors are opaque and outcomes are closed sets, so a blocked read\n     answers `blocked`, a full write answers `full`, and a read parked on a\n     stream that closes resumes to `end` (nil) from its own next poll.\n\n   V2 surface (as v1):\n     (await/go body...)\n     (await/go {:env {'sym val ...}} body...)\n     (await/cursor stream)\n     (await/<! cursor)\n     (await/>! stream value)\n\n   await/cursor / <! / >! are module bindings that return stream effect\n   descriptors. Calls inside a go body are compiled by yang.clojure as\n   ordinary applications; the VM resolves them through the registry value\n   at runtime and the engine dispatches the resulting :stream/cursor,\n   :stream/next, :stream/put effects.\n\n   await/go is a thin syntax+macro layer. It does not introduce a new\n   interpreter, scheduler, or continuation model.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as ds]\n            [yang.clojure :as yang]\n            [yin.vm :as vm]\n            [yin.vm.ast-walker :as ast-walker]\n            [yin.vm.engine :as engine]\n            [yin.vm.module :as module]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Stream effect descriptors: cursor, <!, >!\n;; =============================================================================\n;;\n;; Each function returns an effect descriptor consumed by the yin.vm\n;; engine. They are also registered as module bindings so calls inside a go\n;; body resolve through the registry value at runtime.\n;;\n;; Registered under 'await (matches the doc's recommended :as await alias)\n;; and 'dao.await for fully-qualified callers.\n\n(defn cursor\n  \"Stream cursor effect descriptor. Inside (await/go ...) this produces a\n   :stream/cursor effect handled by the yin.vm engine.\"\n  [stream-ref]\n  (module/make-effect :stream/cursor {:stream stream-ref}))\n\n\n(defn <!\n  \"Stream-read effect descriptor. Inside (await/go ...) this produces a\n   :stream/next effect; the engine returns the value at the cursor or parks\n   the continuation when the transport answers blocked.\"\n  [cursor-ref]\n  (module/make-effect :stream/next {:cursor cursor-ref}))\n\n\n(defn >!\n  \"Stream-write effect descriptor. Inside (await/go ...) this produces a\n   :stream/put effect; the engine appends val to the stream, parking the\n   continuation when the transport answers full.\"\n  [stream-ref val]\n  (module/make-effect :stream/put {:stream stream-ref, :val val}))\n\n\n(def ^:private await-bindings {'cursor cursor, '<! <!, '>! >!})\n\n\n(ns yin.vm\n  \"Canonical data model (Datoms), schema, and primitives for the Yin Abstract\n   Machine on DaoStream v2.\n\n   The shared kernel of `yin.vm` carries over unchanged. What changes is the\n   stream wiring:\n\n   - **The host supplies streams.** v1 hardcoded a ring buffer descriptor and\n     called `ds/open!`. Here a composition hands the interpreter a\n     `:make-stream` constructor exactly as it hands it `:primitives`. There is\n     no default, because a default would smuggle the hardcoded transport back\n     in: absent a supplied constructor, `:stream/make` is unsupported and says\n     so.\n   - **Program observation lives outside the VM.** The program stream handle,\n     cursor, and gap count belong to `dao.stream.observer`; the VM never\n     polls a program stream and no longer accepts `:in-stream` at\n     construction. Language-level stream effects and FFI still operate their\n     own streams and cursors inside the VM.\n   - **Cursors are opaque.** The VM's internal cursor representation is\n     `{:stream-id id :cursor <opaque>}`. There is no position and no seek.\n   - **Construction is all-or-nothing.** Creating the FFI pair and minting its\n     cursors are stream operations with their own outcomes. Any non-`ok`\n     outcome fails construction; a VM cannot be half-built.\n   - **`call-in-cursor-key` is dropped.** v1 wrote it and nothing read it.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [eval])\n  (:require [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.host.integer :as integer-host]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [yin.vm.effect :as effect]\n            [yin.vm.values :as values]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Protocols\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Common VM interfaces implemented by all execution engines.\n\n(defprotocol IVM\n  \"Unified VM protocol for execution, evaluation, and observable state.\"\n\n  (step\n    [vm]\n    \"Execute one step of already-loaded work. An idle VM is returned\n     unchanged; program input arrives through an attached stream observer.\")\n\n  (run\n    [vm]\n    \"Run already-loaded work until halted or blocked.\")\n\n  (eval\n    [vm ast]\n    \"Convert and evaluate a supplied AST. Does not drain any independently\n     queued program input; explicit observer coordination does that.\")\n\n  (reset\n    [vm]\n    \"Reset execution state to a known initial state, preserving loaded program.\")\n\n  (halted?\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns true if VM has halted (completed or error).\")\n\n  (blocked?\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns true if VM is blocked waiting for external input.\")\n\n  (value\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns the current result value, or nil if not yet computed.\"))\n\n\n(defprotocol IVMState\n  \"CESK state accessor protocol.\n   Exposes the four components of the CESK machine model.\n   Representations are VM-specific but the structure is universal.\"\n\n  (control\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns the current control state (what is being evaluated).\n     VM-specific: AST node, instruction pointer, node ID, etc.\")\n\n  (environment\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns the current lexical environment (variable bindings).\")\n\n  (store\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns the current store (heap/global state).\")\n\n  (continuation\n    [vm]\n    \"Returns the current continuation (what to do next).\n     VM-specific: linked frames, stack vector, call-stack, etc.\"))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n(ns dao.space.index\n  \"The transactor-side indexing library (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Publication\n   from an agent).\n\n   In dao.space every agent appending to its own `dao.stream` is its own\n   transactor, so every agent also owns indexing its own datoms. This\n   library is that duty, the write-side peer of `dao.space.query` (the\n   embeddable reader that consumes what this publishes):\n\n     - `publish-index!` snapshots the agent's local stream, builds the four\n       covered indexes as immutable content-addressed `dao.data.btree` node\n       blobs, and appends them (children before parents, manifest last) to\n       one intake stream selected from an explicit pool. A DaoJing observer\n       over the pool materializes the blobs into content-addressed storage.\n     - `read-manifest` / `read-datoms` read a published manifest back and\n       walk its EAVT node graph eagerly; `restored-indexes` re-attaches the\n       manifest's trees lazily.\n\n   It owns the index *realization* both sides share:\n\n     - the sort orders (`eavt-cmp`/`aevt-cmp`/`avet-cmp`/`vaet-cmp` over\n       heterogeneous values) and the in-memory index (`index-datoms`,\n       `subseq-from`)\n     - the persisted node-blob format, both directions: nodes store as\n       plain-EDN content-addressed segment blobs (Merkle by construction —\n       dao.data.btree stores children before parents); the manifest is\n       exactly `{:indexes {:eavt addr-or-nil :aevt ... :avet ... :vaet ...}\n       :count n :branching-factor n}` — no source stream, no pool, no epoch,\n       no own address.\n\n   Build and lazy restore run on every platform: the tree is dao.data.btree,\n   one .cljc source (JVM, cljs, cljd), and durability is its IStorage over a\n   content-store handle (dao.data.btree.storage). Node blobs are ordinary\n   EDN either way.\n\n   The same namespace is also the *stateful* dao.stream observer of\n   docs/design/dao.space.index.as-observer.md: `session` builds the consumer\n   half a `dao.stream.observer/run-on-stream` session drives with\n   `fold-batch`/`flush-staged`, `publish!`/`checkpoint`/`restore` publish and\n   recover it, and `db-value` hands its live trees to `dao.space.query`. The\n   index is a symmetric peer observer there — it never evaluates, and it\n   creates, appends to, and closes no medium.\"\n  (:require [dao.data.btree :as bt]\n            [dao.data.btree.storage :as bts]\n            [dao.datom :as datom]\n            [dao.host.integer :as host-integer]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Value comparison (heterogeneous datom values)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- type-rank\n  \"The heterogeneous bucket of a datom value. Every Jing numeric kind is\n   one bucket (dao.jing.cbor/numeric?, not host number?): the JVM Rational\n   carrier and the JavaScript and Dart float64, decimal and rational\n   carriers are not host numbers, and must sort among numbers by value.\"\n  [x]\n  (cond (nil? x) 0\n        (boolean? x) 1\n        (cbor/numeric? x) 2\n        (string? x) 3\n        (keyword? x) 4\n        (symbol? x) 5\n        :else 6))\n\n\n(defn- native-integer?\n  [x]\n  (and (host-integer/exact-integer? x) (not (host-integer/big-carrier? x))))\n\n\n(defn- compare-numbers\n  \"Portable numeric order (dao.jing.cbor/num-compare: exact, never rounded\n   through double; -infinity < finite < +infinity < NaN, 0.0 and -0.0\n   equal), with host compare kept only for two native exact integers,\n   where every host answers identically. Every float takes num-compare\n   on every host: Dart's double compareTo orders -0.0 below 0.0.\"\n  [a b]\n  (if (and (native-integer? a) (native-integer? b))\n    (compare a b)\n    (cbor/num-compare a b)))\n   an explicit intake pool of dao.stream reader handles and materializes\n   every payload through dao.stream.observe/step. Pool membership and\n   every member's initial cursor are supplied by the caller; statuses and\n   the scheduling index are ordinary immutable data. There are no atoms,\n   globals, registration, or discovery, and the source stream never enters\n   an address or a stored value.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [get])\n  (:require [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n            [dao.host.hash :as hash]\n            [dao.host.text :as text]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.base64 :as stream-base64]\n            [dao.stream.observe :as observe]\n            [dao.jing.cbor :as cbor]))\n\n\n(def ^:private content-missing\n  \"Internal not-found sentinel used to verify :present read-backs. An opaque\n   per-host identity object, never a keyword: :dao.jing/content-missing is a\n   legal opaque payload, and a keyword sentinel would be ambiguous with a\n   genuinely stored value. Never exposed: a conforming backend cannot store\n   or return it.\"\n  #?(:cljd (Object.)\n     :clj (Object.)\n     :cljs (js-obj)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Content addressing (docs/design/dao.jing.md, Canonical encoding)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n\n\n(def ^:private hex-digits \"0123456789abcdef\")\n\n\n(defn- bytes->hex\n  \"Lowercase hex string of host bytes bs.\"\n  [bs]\n  (let [n (bytes/length bs)\n        sb #?(:cljd (StringBuffer.)\n              :clj (StringBuilder.)\n              :cljs (js/Array.))\n        append! #?(:cljd (fn [^String s] (.write ^StringBuffer sb s))\n                   :clj (fn [^String s] (.append ^StringBuilder sb s))\n   Host contact goes through the primitive profile\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, slice H2): big\n   integers through `dao.host.integer`, the Float64, Decimal and Rational\n   carriers through `dao.host.number`, float bits and fields through\n   `dao.host.float`, byte arrays through `dao.host.bytes` and strict\n   UTF-8 through `dao.host.text`; a profile refusal is answered as this\n   codec's refusal of the same class.\"\n  (:refer-clojure :exclude [decimal? ratio?])\n  (:require\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    [dao.host.float :as float]\n    [dao.host.integer :as integer]\n    [dao.host.number :as number]\n    [dao.host.text :as text]))\n\n\n;; ==========================================================================\n;; Refusals\n;; ==========================================================================\n\n(defn- refuse\n  \"Throw the refusal `cls` (a corpus refusal class keyword).\"\n  ([cls] (refuse cls nil))\n  ([cls detail]\n   (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.jing.cbor refused: \" (name cls)\n                        (when detail (str \" (\" detail \")\")))\n                   {::refusal cls :detail detail}))))\n\n\n(defn- type-name\n  \"The host type of x, for refusal details.\"\n  [x]\n  #?(:cljd (str (.-runtimeType x))\n     :default (str (type x))))\n\n\n(defn refusal\n  \"The refusal class keyword of a thrown codec refusal, or nil. A refusal\n   the `dao.host` profile throws outside any codec call (on JS, the\n   `Float64` carrier refusing implicit numeric coercion) carries the same\n   corpus class under `:dao.host/refusal` and reads the same here.\"\n  [ex]\n  (let [d (ex-data ex)]\n    (or (::refusal d) (:dao.host/refusal d))))\n\n\n(defn- host\n  \"(f & args) through the primitive profile: a `dao.host` refusal comes\n   back as this codec's refusal of the same class (the profile reuses the\n   corpus refusal vocabulary).\"\n  [f & args]\n  (try\n    (apply f args)\n    (catch #?(:cljd Object :clj Exception :cljs :default) e\n      (if-let [cls (:dao.host/refusal (ex-data e))]\n        (refuse cls (:detail (ex-data e)))\nzsh:1: no matches found: src/clj/dao/host/*\n","exit_code":1,"status":"failed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'retention|cursor|defn next|read|readers' src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | head -55 && sed -n '1,105p' src/cljc/dao/star/facts.cljc && sed -n '1,50p' src/cljc/dao/star/router.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'retention|cursor|defn next|read|readers' src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc | head -55 && sed -n '1,105p' src/cljc/dao/star/facts.cljc && sed -n '1,50p' src/cljc/dao/star/router.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"25:  ;; The logical identity is carried in descriptors and cursors.  It therefore\n55:  (cursor\n62:                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n66:                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n73:    [_ cursor-value]\n76:        (not (map? cursor-value)) (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n77:        (not (contains? cursor-value :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity))\n78:        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n79:        (not= (:identity s) (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity cursor-value))\n80:        (result :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch)\n81:        (not (integer? (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)))\n82:        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n84:        (let [pos (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)\n90:                   :dao.stream/cursor\n97:                    ;; past that tail is correct; the next read answers `end`.\n104:             :dao.stream/cursor {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n(ns dao.star.facts\n  \"The session-facts grammar of dao.star Mode 1 (docs/design/dao.star.md,\n   Sessions and request kinds), as pure functions.\n\n   Three facts are written, only through the constructors here: a lineage's\n   root, a consumption (one transition of one lineage), and the end of a\n   lineage.  `valid-fact?` guards readers: anything else on the medium (lease\n   renewals, foreign values) is noise to be ignored, never thrown on.\n\n   `index-step` folds one fact, or one lineage-lease record, into the\n   occurrence -> lineage -> current index a router materializes.  The index is\n   derived state, rebuilt from the media; nothing here keeps a table.\")\n\n\n(defn root\n  \"A lineage's first fact: its id is its root occurrence's id.\"\n  [lineage]\n  {:dao.star/lineage lineage :dao.star/occurrence lineage})\n\n\n(defn consumption\n  \"One transition: `successor` replaces `predecessor` as the lineage's\n   current occurrence.\"\n  [lineage successor predecessor]\n  {:dao.star/lineage lineage\n   :dao.star/occurrence successor\n   :dao.star/predecessor predecessor})\n\n\n(defn ended\n  \"The lineage's program finished (`reason`, a keyword such as :halted).\"\n  [lineage reason]\n  {:dao.star/lineage lineage :dao.star/ended reason})\n\n\n(defn- id?\n  [x]\n  (and (string? x) (not (empty? x))))\n\n\n(defn fact-kind\n  \"`:root`, `:consumption`, `:ended`, or nil for anything else.  Facts have\n   exactly the keys the constructors produce; a value with more is not one.\"\n  [x]\n  (when (map? x)\n    (let [ks (set (keys x))\n          lineage (:dao.star/lineage x)\n          occurrence (:dao.star/occurrence x)]\n      (cond\n        (not (id? lineage)) nil\n\n        (and (= ks #{:dao.star/lineage :dao.star/occurrence})\n             (= lineage occurrence))\n        :root\n\n        (and (= ks #{:dao.star/lineage :dao.star/occurrence :dao.star/predecessor})\n             (id? occurrence)\n             (id? (:dao.star/predecessor x))\n             (not= occurrence (:dao.star/predecessor x)))\n        :consumption\n\n        (and (= ks #{:dao.star/lineage :dao.star/ended})\n             (keyword? (:dao.star/ended x)))\n        :ended\n\n        :else nil))))\n\n\n(defn valid-fact?\n  [x]\n  (some? (fact-kind x)))\n\n\n(def empty-index\n  {:occurrence {}    ; occurrence -> lineage\n   :current {}       ; lineage -> its current occurrence\n   :ended #{}        ; lineages that are over (halted or lapsed)\n   :leases {}})      ; lease id -> lineage, from lineage-lease grants\n\n\n(defn- lineage-subject\n  [fact]\n  (let [s (:dao.lease/subject fact)]\n    (when (and (map? s) (id? (:dao.star/lineage s)))\n      (:dao.star/lineage s))))\n\n\n(defn index-step\n  \"Fold one read value into the index.  A valid session fact advances it; a\n   lineage-lease `:accepted` records which lineage a lease is for and a\n   `:lapsed` ends that lineage for routing; anything else, and any fact that\n   does not follow from the index (a consumption whose predecessor is not\n   current), changes nothing.\"\n  [index x]\n  (case (fact-kind x)\n    :root (let [s (:dao.star/lineage x)]\n            (if (contains? (:current index) s)\n              index\n              (-> index\n                  (assoc-in [:current s] s)\n                  (assoc-in [:occurrence s] s))))\n\n    :consumption (let [s (:dao.star/lineage x)\n                       o2 (:dao.star/occurrence x)]\n                   (if (and (= (:dao.star/predecessor x) (get-in index [:current s]))\n(ns dao.star.router\n  \"The dao.star Mode 1 router (docs/design/dao.star.md, Routing and Sessions\n   and request kinds).\n\n   A stepped interpreter: each pass reads the ingress with its own cursor to\n   `blocked`, then drains the session-facts and lineage-lease cursors to\n   `blocked` folding them into the index, and only then classifies what the\n   ingress gave it.  Unknown is judged after both drains, never before, which\n   is what makes a just-published token impossible to answer as begin.\n\n   The router holds no token table of its own: the index is derived from the\n   two media it reads, and it forwards a request to the medium of the session\n   its token names, with every later terminal event of that exchange.  The\n   boundary never routes, and a gap forwards nothing.\"\n  (:require [dao.star.facts :as facts]\n            [dao.star.lease :as lease]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            #?(:clj [dao.stream.remote]))\n  #?(:clj (:import [dao.stream.remote ReflectionHandle])))\n\n\n(defn- reflection?\n  [handle]\n  #?(:clj (instance? ReflectionHandle handle)\n     :default (and false handle)))\n\n\n(defn classify\n  \"Pure: the kind of request `(method, path)` under `index`.\n     {:kind :begin}                         `/`, or a token of an ended or\n                                            unknown lineage\n     {:kind :subscribe :lineage S}          `/s/<token>`, token live\n     {:kind :input :lineage S :token t}     `/k/<token>`, token current\n     {:kind :stale :lineage S :token t}     `/k/<token>`, token consumed\n     {:kind :unrouted}                      any other path\"\n  [index path]\n  (let [[_ head token] (re-matches #\"/(s|k)/([^/]+)\" (str path))\n        tok (when head (facts/resolve-token index token))]\n    (cond\n      (= \"/\" path) {:kind :begin}\n      (nil? head) {:kind :unrouted}\n      (contains? #{:ended :unknown} (:kind tok)) {:kind :begin}\n      (= \"s\" head) {:kind :subscribe :lineage (:lineage tok) :token token}\n      (= :current (:kind tok)) {:kind :input :lineage (:lineage tok) :token token}\n      :else {:kind :stale :lineage (:lineage tok) :token token})))\n\n\n(defn make-router\n  \"Assemble the router.  Config:\n     :ingress        the boundary's ingress medium (may be a reflection)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'hashUnencodedChars|hashInt\\\\(o.hashCode|hasheq\\\\(Object' /Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src 2>/dev/null | head -20 && nl -ba docker/dao-star-cart/README.md && nl -ba resources/dao_star_cart/README.md && sed -n '1,80p' src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc && sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_26","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'hashUnencodedChars|hashInt\\\\(o.hashCode|hasheq\\\\(Object' /Users/sto/.m2/repository/org/clojure/clojure /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/src 2>/dev/null | head -20 && nl -ba docker/dao-star-cart/README.md && nl -ba resources/dao_star_cart/README.md && sed -n '1,80p' src/cljc/dao/host/hash.cljc && sed -n '1,120p' src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t# dao.star cart: run note\n     2\t\n     3\t- Port **3004** in the container; map `3004:3004` (a separate compose file or\n     4\t  `docker run`; do not touch the lambdacart compose project).\n     5\t- `CATALOG_PATH` (default `/data/catalog.edn`) must be on a **volume**. On first\n     6\t  boot with an empty volume the baked seed `catalog.edn` is copied there; every\n     7\t  grid save rewrites it atomically. If the path is unwritable the demo logs it\n     8\t  and keeps editing in memory. Edits in a running container never touch the repo\n     9\t  copy of `catalog.edn`.\n    10\t- Images are baked from the build context (`resources/dao_star_cart/public/images/`,\n    11\t  68 MB, gitignored). Alternatively mount that directory read-only over\n    12\t  `/app/resources/dao_star_cart/public/images` and set `ASSET_ROOT` if the layout differs.\n    13\t- `deps.edn` here is trimmed (no datomic, postgres, antlr, jsoup, stigmergy/chp):\n    14\t  the demo's namespace closure was checked to load none of them.\n    15\t- Run limits on shiva: `docker run --memory 1g --cpus 1`; build with BuildKit and\n    16\t  `docker build` only. Do not run `bb` lanes or the repo test suites there.\n    17\t- **No authentication on `/grid`: do not expose this port beyond the private\n    18\t  interface.**\n     1\t# dao.star cart\n     2\t\n     3\tA reproduction of the functionality of the lambdacart shop on dao.star Mode 1,\n     4\twith dao.space as the database and `dao.space.query/q` for every query. The UI\n     5\tis its own minimal dark theme; no lambdacart code is reused.\n     6\t\n     7\t**Shop** (`/`): catalog grid, item detail, add/remove, cart with total, checkout\n     8\tstub (no payment). The cart is a local of the session's yin.vm continuation and\n     9\tlives as long as the lineage lease.\n    10\t\n    11\t**Grid** (`/grid`): catalog editor. Edit name, price (minor units), description;\n    12\tadd/remove images (existing `/images/` files only) and tagories. Every mutation\n    13\tgoes through a host effects interpreter that validates it, transacts it, and\n    14\tappends `{:saved ..}` / `{:rejected ..}` back to the session; the UI shows\n    15\t`saving` until then. Each save rewrites `catalog.edn` (atomic rename).\n    16\t\n    17\t## Read this\n    18\t\n    19\t- **No authentication on the grid. Do not expose it.** The session token\n    20\t  identifies a session; it is not authorization.\n    21\t- **Coherence is stale-until-navigation.** A storefront view shows the catalog as\n    22\t  of its last projection; a grid save is visible after the shopper navigates or\n    23\t  reloads. There is no broadcast; the shop footer says so.\n    24\t- **A subscription ends once per hour** (the exchange lease's `:dao.lease/max`)\n    25\t  and the browser reconnects, which re-projects the current cart.\n    26\t- **`public/js/datastar.js` must be vendored** at the pinned version\n    27\t  (`dao.star-cart.view/datastar-version`, 1.0.0-RC.7) before the pages work in a\n    28\t  browser: `sh resources/dao_star_cart/vendor-datastar.sh`.\n    29\t\n    30\t## Provenance\n    31\t\n    32\t`catalog.edn` was extracted from the live lambdacart Datomic on 2026-10-26 (see\n    33\tthe header comment in that file for the form): 249 items including two empty\n    34\tplaceholder records present in the source. Prices are minor units. Product images\n    35\t(`public/images/`, 228 files, 68 MB) belong to the shop and are gitignored;\n    36\tnothing here is to be published without the owner's say-so.\n(ns dao.host.hash\n  \"The cryptographic hash functions of the primitive profile\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\n\n   P entries (§2.4): `blake3-bytes` and `sha256-bytes`. They take host\n   bytes (byte[] on JVM, Uint8Array on JS, Uint8List on Dart) and return\n   fresh host bytes representing the 32-byte digest (256 bits). They leave\n   their arguments unmodified.\n\n   String digesting is a table B portable composition over `dao.host.text`\n   and these byte primitives (`blake3`, `sha256`, `digest-string`,\n   `digest-bytes`), hosted in `dao.jing`.\"\n  (:require\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    #?@(:cljd [[\"package:blake3_dart/blake3_dart.dart\" :as blake3-dart]\n               [\"package:crypto/crypto.dart\" :as crypto]])\n    #?@(:cljs [[\"@noble/hashes/blake3.js\" :as noble-blake3]\n               [\"@noble/hashes/sha2.js\" :as noble-sha256]]))\n  #?(:cljd (:import [\"dart:typed_data\" Uint8List])\n     :clj  (:import [io.github.rctcwyvrn.blake3 Blake3]\n                    [java.security MessageDigest])))\n\n\n(defn- check-bytes!\n  [entry bs]\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.host refused: \" entry \" unsupported-value (expected host bytes)\")\n                    {:dao.host/refusal :unsupported-value\n                     :dao.host/entry entry}))))\n\n\n(defn blake3-bytes\n  \"P. 32-byte BLAKE3 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\"\n  [bs]\n  (check-bytes! 'blake3-bytes bs)\n  #?(:cljd (let [^Uint8List u8 (if (instance? Uint8List bs)\n                                 bs\n                                 (Uint8List.fromList bs))]\n             (Uint8List.fromList (blake3-dart/blake3 u8)))\n     :clj (let [b (Blake3/newInstance)]\n            (.update b ^bytes bs)\n            (.digest b))\n     :cljs (let [out (noble-blake3/blake3 bs)]\n             (js/Uint8Array.from out))))\n\n\n(defn sha256-bytes\n  \"P. 32-byte SHA-256 digest of host bytes `bs`, returning fresh host bytes.\"\n  [bs]\n  (check-bytes! 'sha256-bytes bs)\n  #?(:cljd (let [digest (.convert crypto/sha256 bs)]\n             (Uint8List.fromList (.-bytes digest)))\n     :clj (let [md (MessageDigest/getInstance \"SHA-256\")]\n            (.digest md ^bytes bs))\n     :cljs (let [out (noble-sha256/sha256 bs)]\n             (js/Uint8Array.from out))))\n(ns dao.stream.ringbuffer\n  \"Fixed-capacity, evict-oldest DaoStream v2 reference transport.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream :as stream]))\n\n\n(def transport-type :dao.stream/ringbuffer)\n(def capacity-key :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity)\n\n\n(defn- result\n  [outcome]\n  {:dao.stream/outcome outcome})\n\n\n(defn- valid-spec?\n  [spec]\n  (and (map? spec)\n       (= transport-type (:dao.stream/type spec))\n       (integer? (get spec capacity-key))\n       (pos? (get spec capacity-key))))\n\n\n(defn- fresh-state\n  [capacity]\n  ;; The logical identity is carried in descriptors and cursors.  It therefore\n  ;; belongs to DaoStream's portable data domain, unlike a host UUID object.\n  (atom {:identity (str (random-uuid))\n         :capacity capacity\n         :first 0\n         :tail 0\n         :values {}\n         :closed? false\n         :attachments {}}))\n\n\n(declare attach!)\n\n\n(deftype RingHandle\n  [state attachment-id owner?]\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamDescriptor\n\n  (descriptor\n    [_]\n    (let [s @state]\n      {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n       :dao.stream/descriptor {:dao.stream/type transport-type\n                               :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}\n       :dao.stream/identity (:identity s)}))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamReader\n\n  (cursor\n    [_ anchor]\n    (let [s @state]\n      (if (and (not owner?) (not (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :open?])))\n        (result :dao.stream/closed)\n        (case anchor\n          :dao.stream/oldest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:first s)})\n          :dao.stream/newest (assoc (result :dao.stream/ok)\n                                    :dao.stream/cursor\n                                    {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                     :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:tail s)})\n          (result :dao.stream/invalid-anchor)))))\n\n\n  (next\n    [_ cursor-value]\n    (let [s @state]\n      (cond\n        (not (map? cursor-value)) (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        (not (contains? cursor-value :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity))\n        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        (not= (:identity s) (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity cursor-value))\n        (result :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch)\n        (not (integer? (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)))\n        (result :dao.stream/invalid-cursor)\n        :else\n        (let [pos (:dao.stream.ringbuffer/position cursor-value)\n              frozen (when-not owner? (get-in s [:attachments attachment-id :tail]))\n              visible-tail (if (some? frozen) frozen (:tail s))]\n          (cond\n            (< pos (:first s))\n            (assoc (result :dao.stream/gap)\n                   :dao.stream/cursor\n                   {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                    ;; The contract's recovery position is the earliest retained\n                    ;; position, which equals the tail when nothing is retained.\n                    ;; It must never be an evicted position: a frozen attachment\n                    ;; whose visible history is fully evicted would otherwise be\n                    ;; handed its own tail back and re-gap forever.  Recovering\n                    ;; past that tail is correct; the next read answers `end`.\n                    :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (:first s)})\n            (< pos visible-tail)\n            ;; `:first`, `:tail`, and `:values` are updated as one state value;\n            ;; positions in this interval are always retained by the transport.\n            {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok\n             :dao.stream/value (get (:values s) pos)\n             :dao.stream/cursor {:dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity (:identity s)\n                                 :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position (inc pos)}}\n            (or (:closed? s) (some? frozen)) (result :dao.stream/end)\n            :else (result :dao.stream/blocked))))))\n\n\n  stream/IDaoStreamWriter\n\n  (append!\n    [_ value]\n    ;; Check attachment liveness *inside* the one state transition.  A deref\n    ;; before swap! permits an append that began before an attachment close to\n    ;; land after it; this form gives append!/close! a single linearization point.\n    (let [out (volatile! nil)]\n      (swap! state\n             (fn [s]\n               (if (or (:closed? s)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
